   * product over k. Because 'data' is row-major, accessing
   * data[k][i] and data[k][j] is strided in memory.
   *
   * We reformulate this as:
   *
   *   cov = sum_{k=0}^{N-1} ( data[k]^T * data[k] )
   *
   * i.e., a sum of outer products of each centered row with
   * itself. We implement:
   *
   *   - cov is initialized to zero.
   *   - For each row k:
   *       for each i:
   *         for each j >= i:
   *           cov[i][j] += data[k][i] * data[k][j];
   *
   * For each fixed (i,j), the arithmetic sequence is:
   *   cov[i][j] = 0;
   *   for k = 0..N-1:
   *     cov[i][j] += data[k][i] * data[k][j];
   *
   * i.e., the same accumulation order over k as in the
   * original code; only operations for different (i,j) pairs
   * are interleaved.
   *
   * We parallelize over 'i' for each row 'k' using OpenMP:
   *   - For a given k, '#pragma omp for' distributes i across
   *     threads. Each (i,j) pair is updated by exactly one
   *     thread for that k.
   *   - There is an implicit barrier at the end of each
   *     'omp for', so all contributions for k complete before
   *     any thread starts k+1. Hence each cov[i][j] sees its
   *     updates in increasing k order, as in the original.
   *   - We only accumulate the upper triangle (j >= i). The
   *     lower triangle is filled by symmetry afterwards.
   * ---------------------------------------------------------- */

  const DATA_TYPE denom = float_n - SCALAR_VAL(1.0);

  /* Zero-initialize full covariance matrix once.
     (Original code initialized only cov[i][j] before use; the
      lower triangle is unused until it is written from the
      upper triangle, so zeroing it here is harmless.) */
  for (i = 0; i < mPB; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < mPB; j++)
